e non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{3}\\) must contain the remaining two points of \\(P\\).\n\nWe now examine all possibilities for the non\u2011sunny line \\(L_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  \\(L_{3}\\) vertical.\n\n*Case \\(x=1\\):*  contains three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe three points left are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nEvery pair among them has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)), so none can be placed on a sunny line.  \nThus this case is impossible.\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\):*  contains two points \\((2,1),(2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\).  \nThe only sunny pair among these four is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \nThe other two points would have to form a sunny pair, but \\((1,1)\\) with either \\((1,3)\\) (vertical) or \\((3,1)\\) (horizontal) is forbidden.  \nHence impossible.\n\n*Case \\(x=3\\):*  contains a single point \\((3,1)\\).  \nThen five points must be covered by the two sunny lines, which is impossible because each sunny line can cover at most two points (Lemma\u202f2). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  \\(L_{3}\\) horizontal.\n\nThe analysis is symmetric to the vertical case.\n\n*Case \\(y=1\\):*  three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) are on \\(L_{3}\\); the three remaining points \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) cannot be split into two sunny lines (every pair has a forbidden slope).\n\n*Case \\(y=2\\):*  two points \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) on \\(L_{3}\\); the four remaining points \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) admit only one sunny pair \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)), leaving an impossible second pair.\n\n*Case \\(y=3\\):*  a single point \\((1,3)\\) on \\(L_{3}\\); the other five points cannot be covered by two sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  \\(L_{3}\\) parallel to \\(x+y=0