tical, i.e. parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, which is forbidden for a sunny line.  \nSimilarly, two distinct points \\((a_{1},1)\\) and \\((a_{2},1)\\) force the line to be horizontal.  \nFinally, a line through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \nThus a sunny line cannot contain two different points of \\(B_{n}\\). ∎\n\n\n\nA non‑sunny line can be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nAmong the points of \\(B_{n}\\) a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (or a horizontal line \\(y=1\\)) contains exactly \\(n\\) points, while a line of slope \\(-1\\) contains at most one point of \\(B_{n}\\).  \nHence a single non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(B_{n}\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) uses at most one non‑sunny line.  \nThen, by Lemma 1, at least \\(2n-1-n=n-1\\) sunny lines are required to cover the remaining points of \\(B_{n}\\).  \nThus the total number of lines needed is at least  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})\\;+\\;(n-1)\\;(\\text{sunny})=n .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are already exhausted, leaving **no** line to cover any interior point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \nFor \\(n\\ge4\\) such interior points exist (their number is \\(\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2>0\\)), a contradiction.  \n\nConsequently **at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary** when \\(n\\ge4\\).  \nSince the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2\\qquad (n\\ge4).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the interior consists of the single point \\((2,2)\\); a direct inspection (see the construction below) shows that the maximal possible \\(k\\) is also \\(1=n-2\\).  \nHence for every \\(n\\ge3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence of configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\\(\\mathcal P_{3}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\).\n\n* \\(k=0\\): use the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=3 .\n  \\]\n\n* \\(k=1\\): keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) and add the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L_{3}:y=2x-2\\quad(\\text{slope }2).\n  \\]\n\nBoth families consist of three distinct lines and cover all four points, with respectively \\(0\\) and \\(1\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\); i.e. for every  \n\n\\[\nk'\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,(n-1)-2=n-3\\}\n\\]\n\nthere is a configuration of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nwith exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\nNow consider \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\).  \nTake the two non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) that lie on the first column or the first row.  \nThe set of points that remain uncovered is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_{n}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}\n   =\\{(a',b')+(1,1)\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal Q_{n}\\) is a translate of \\(\\mathcal P_{n-1}\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis we can cover \\(\\mathcal Q_{n}\\) with the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines, using any prescribed number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines where \\(0\\le k'\\le n-3\\).  \nAdding \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) (both non‑sunny) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) and containing exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=k' \\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.\n\nFinally, the value \\(k=n-2\\) is obtained by choosing \\(k'=n-3\\) in the inductive construction and then **replacing** one of the two fixed non‑sunny lines by a sunny line that still passes through all points it previously covered.  \nFor instance, replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\); this line contains the point \\((1,1)\\) and all points \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) are already covered by the other lines of the inductive configuration, so the covering property is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one.  \nHence the value \\(k=n-2\\) is also attainable.\n\nThus for the given \\(n\\) every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realized.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the constructive proof that every integer in the interval \\([0,n-2]\\) is attainable, we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge3,\\;k\\text{ can be any integer }0\\le k\\le n-2.}\n\\]"
