n\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the translation \\(\\tau:(a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\).  \nThen \\(\\tau(\\mathcal Q_n)=\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}\\); indeed,\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;(a-1)+(b-1)\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nApply the construction of Section 2 to the integer \\(n-2\\): we obtain the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\ntogether with the horizontal line \\(y=2\\).  \nAfter translating back by \\(\\tau^{-1}\\) these lines cover exactly the set \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\); all of them are non‑sunny.\n\nNow replace the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{sun}}:\\;y=2x-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_{\\text{sun}}\\) passes through the point \\((2,2)\\in\\mathcal Q_n\\) and also through \\((1,0)\\), which is irrelevant.  \nSince \\((2,2)\\) is the only point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) that lies on \\(y=2\\), the replacement does not leave any point uncovered: every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) other than \\((2,2)\\) is still covered by one of the vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;y=2x-2\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains exactly **one** sunny line.  \n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved rigorously that\n\n* for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\);\n* for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.\n\nThe determination of whether larger values of \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)) can be realised remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify 