s the contributions of different lines as if they were disjoint. However, distinct non\u2011sunny lines may cover the *same* point(s) of \\(D_n\\). For example, the vertical line \\(x=1\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=n\\) both contain the point \\((1,n)\\) of \\(D_n\\). Therefore the inequality  \n\\[\nn\\le k + s\\,n\n\\]\ndoes **not** follow from Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2; the correct bound on the *number of distinct* points of \\(D_n\\) covered is at most  \n\\[\nk + \\bigl(\\text{number of non\u2011sunny lines that are the diagonal}\\bigr) + (s- \\text{that number})\\cdot 1,\n\\]\nwhich is far weaker than the claimed additive bound. Consequently the subsequent inequality is not justified.\n\n#### 4.2 Algebraic rearrangement\n\n> \u201cRearranging gives  \n> \\[\n> k\\le n-2 .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (algebraic mistake).**  \nEven if we (incorrectly) accept the inequality \\(n\\le k+(n-k)n\\), solving for \\(k\\) yields:\n\\[\nn\\le k + n^2 - kn \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; (n-1)k \\le n^2 - n = n(n-1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; k\\le n,\n\\]\nnot \\(k\\le n-2\\). The step \u201cRearranging gives \\(k\\le n-2\\)\u201d is mathematically false. Hence the claimed upper bound is invalid.\n\n#### 4.3 Consequence\n\n> \u201cThus any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).\u201d\n\nBecause the derivation of the bound is flawed, this conclusion is unsupported. The statement is therefore **incorrect**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) consider the following \\(n\\) lines:  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1),\\qquad\\qquad\n> L_n:\\;y=1 .\n> \\]  \n> *Coverage.* \u2026 *Sunny count.* All the lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis or the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. Hence we have exhibited a configuration with \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) cover every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a