ssertion \u201c\\((2,2)\\) is the only point of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) that lies on \\(y=2\\)\u201d is false for \\(n\\ge4\\). For example, \\((3,2), (4,2),\\dots,(n-1,2)\\) also belong to \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) and lie on the line \\(y=2\\). The argument that the replacement leaves no point uncovered therefore lacks a correct justification.  \n\nThe correct reasoning is that the vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) already cover all points of \\(\\mathcal Q_n\\) (including those with \\(b=2\\)), so the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) is redundant and may be replaced by any distinct line, sunny or not. This reasoning is not provided, leaving a gap in the proof.\n\n#### 6.5 Counting the lines\n\n> \u201cConsequently the family  \n> \\[\n> \\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\;y=2x-2\\,\\}\n> \\]  \n> consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains exactly **one** sunny line.\u201d\n\n*Correct* (once the missing justification is supplied).  The set indeed contains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (the line \\(y=2x-2\\)) is sunny, and the union of the lines covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). However, because the justification for the replacement is incomplete, the construction as presented is not fully rigorous.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Conclusion\n\n> \u201cWe have proved rigorously that  \n> * for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\);  \n> * for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.  \n> The determination of whether larger values of \\(k\\) (i.e. \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\)) can be realised remains open.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Critical Error (incomplete solution).**  \nThe problem explicitly asks to *determine all* non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which such a configuration exists. By providing only the (incorrect) upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) and exhibiting constructions for \\(k=0,1\\), the solution leaves the range \\(2\\le k\\le n