 \\(r= -1\\); Step C is omitted and we use the following \\(n\\) lines:\n   \\[\n   L_1,\\;L_2,\\;S_1,\\dots ,S_{n-1}.\n   \\]\n   The same verification as above works because the vertical lines are no longer needed: for any \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).\n\n   When \\(k=n\\) we discard the two non‑sunny lines and keep only the sunny family\n   \\[\n   S_1,\\;S_2,\\dots ,S_n .\n   \\]\n   For each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(S_a\\) contains **all** points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(a\\); consequently the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.  \n   All \\(S_a\\) are sunny because their slopes are \\(\\dfrac{n-a-1}{a-1}\\) (with the convention \\(S_1:y=x\\)), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nD_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(D_n\\) consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  No restriction other than \\(k\\le n\\)\n\nIf a line contains two distinct points of \\(D_n\\) then its slope is \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(D_n\\).  \nSince \\(|D_n|=n\\), any family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can contain at most \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nThus the only a priori bound is  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall build a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The backbone (always present)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1 ,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny.  They cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that belong to the first row or the first column.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Sunny spokes\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-1 = m_i\\,(x-1),\\qquad \nm_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & i=1,\\\\[4pt]\n\\dfrac{n+1-i-1}{\\,i-1\\,}, & i\\ge 2 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S_i\\) passes through the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  \nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le k\\le n\\),\n\n* \\(m_i\\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(n+1-i-1=0\\Rightarrow i=n\\), which would give slope \\(0\\) only when \\(i=n\\) and \\(k=n\\); in that case we replace \\(S_n\\) by the line \\(y=x\\), which is sunny);\n* \\(m_i\\neq\\infty\\) (the line is not vertical);\n* \\(m_i\\neq -1\\) (the equality \\(n+1-i-1=-(i-1)\\) would imply \\(n=1\\), impossible).\n\nHence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Vertical fillers (only when needed)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nr = n-2-k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r\\ge 0\\) (i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\)) we add the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j ,\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,r+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny and distinct from \\(L_1\\) because their abscissae differ from \\(1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\n*If \\(k\\le n-2\\):*  \nthe family consists of the two backbone lines (7), the \\(k\\) sunny spokes (8) and the \\(r=n-2-k\\) vertical fillers (10).  \nHence the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2 + k + (n-2-k)=n .\n\\]\n\n*If \\(k=n-1\\):*  \n\\(r=-1\\); we omit the vertical fillers and keep the two backbone lines together with the \\(n-1\\) sunny spokes.  \nAgain we have \\(2+(n-1)=n\\) lines.\n\n*If \\(k=n\\):*  \nwe discard the two backbone lines and keep only the \\(n\\) sunny spokes \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\); their number is \\(n\\).\n\nThus in every case we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of coverage\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\); if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).*\n\nAssume now \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).  \nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Case 1 – \\(a\\le r+1\\) (this case occurs only when \\(r\\ge 0\\)).*  \nThen the point lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) defined in (10).\n\n*Case 2 – \\(a\\ge r+2\\).*  \nSet \\(i=a\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(b = n+1-i - t\\) for some integer \\(t\\ge 0\\).  \nBy construction, the sunny line \\(S_i\\) (8) contains all points of the form \\((i,\\,n+1-i-t)\\) with \\(t\\ge 0\\) as long as the second coordinate stays positive; in particular it contains \\((i,b)\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(S_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.6  Number of sunny lines\n\nThe only sunny lines are the \\(k\\) spokes \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\); all other lines are vertical or horizontal and therefore non‑sunny.  \nConsequently the constructed family contains **exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in §1, this shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every \\(n\\ge 3\\)."
