            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nAll non‑negative integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}\n\\]\n\nare attainable; for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For a positive integer \\(t\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=t\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the “anti‑diagonal’’ of sum \\(t\\)).  \n   The set of points required in the statement is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1} D_t .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can contain **at most one** point of any anti‑diagonal \\(D_t\\).  \n   Hence, if an anti‑diagonal is **not** taken as a non‑sunny line, all its \\(|D_t|=t-1\\) points must be supplied by sunny lines, and at least \\(|D_t|\\) sunny lines are needed.  \n   The largest anti‑diagonal is \\(D_{n+1}\\) with \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\); therefore we need at most \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) *largest* anti‑diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-k+2}\n   \\]\n\n   as non‑sunny lines (they are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   These lines cover every point whose sum is at least \\(n-k+2\\).  \n   The remaining points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_k=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{k+1} D_t ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the triangular set with the bound \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  \n   Thus the original problem for the pair \\((n,k)\\) is reduced to the following sub‑problem:\n\n   > **Sub‑problem.** For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) construct \\(m\\) *distinct sunny* lines that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\n   Solving the sub‑problem yields a configuration for the original \\((n,k)\\): the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from the sub‑problem.\n\n4. **Inductive construction for the sub‑problem.**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that such a family of \\(m\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   *Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n   \\(\\mathcal P_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The single line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\; y=2x-1\n   \\]\n\n   passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that for some \\(m\\ge 1\\) we have distinct sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(m)}_1,\\dots ,L^{(m)}_{m}\n   \\]\n\n   covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).  \n   Consider \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\). Its outer anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{m+2}=\\{(i,m+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains \\(m+1\\) points.  \n   Each of the already constructed \\(m\\) lines meets \\(D_{m+2}\\) in at most one point, so at most \\(m\\) points of \\(D_{m+2}\\) are already covered.  \n   Choose a point of \\(D_{m+2}\\) that is **not** covered; for definiteness take \\((m+1,1)\\).  \n\n   Select a slope \\(\\lambda\\) satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   \\lambda\\neq\\text{slope}(L^{(m)}_i)\\;(1\\le i\\le m).\n   \\]\n\n   Such a \\(\\lambda\\) exists because the set of forbidden slopes is finite.  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{m+1}:\\; y-1=\\lambda\\,(x-(m+1)).\n   \\]\n\n   \\(L_{m+1}\\) passes through the uncovered point \\((m+1,1)\\), is sunny by the choice of \\(\\lambda\\), and is distinct from the previous lines.  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{(m+1)}_1,\\dots ,L^{(m+1)}_{m+1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the old \\(m\\) lines together with \\(L_{m+1}\\)) therefore covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{m+1}\\).  \n   This completes the induction.\n\n   Hence for every \\(m\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For the original integer \\(n\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):\n\n   * use the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+1,\\,x+y=n,\\dots ,x+y=n-k+2\\) (all non‑sunny);\n   * apply the inductive construction with \\(m=k\\) to obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the remaining points \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines.\n\n   Therefore every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) is attainable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\text{ are possible}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
