            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nAll non‑negative integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n}\n\\]\n\nare attainable: for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=t\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the “anti‑diagonal’’ of sum \\(t\\)).  \n   The required set of points is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line can meet each anti‑diagonal \\(D_t\\) in **at most one** point (its slope is different from \\(-1\\)).  \n   Consequently, if the diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) is **not** used, the \\(t-1\\) points of \\(D_t\\) must be supplied by sunny lines, and at least \\(t-1\\) sunny lines are necessary.  \n   Hence, if the largest omitted diagonal has index \\(t_{\\max}\\), we need at least \\(t_{\\max}-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ge t_{\\max}-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad n-k\\ge n+1-t_{\\max},\n   \\]\n\n   which together give \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Thus the interval \\([0,n]\\) is the only possible one.\n\n3. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and therefore lies on the corresponding line.  All lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), so they are not sunny.\n\n4. **Construction for a general \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n)\\).**  \n   *Step 4.1 – non‑sunny diagonal lines.*  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) **largest** anti‑diagonals, i.e. the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n-k+2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny and together cover every point whose sum is at least \\(n-k+2\\).\n\n   *Step 4.2 – the remaining points.*  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_k=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{k+1}D_t ,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the triangular set with the bound \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n   *Step 4.3 – a transversal of sunny lines for \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).*  \n   We prove the following lemma.\n\n   > **Lemma (Sunny transversal).**  \n   > For every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\n   The lemma gives, for \\(m=k\\), a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the set (2).  Adding these \\(k\\) lines to the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (1) yields a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n5. **Proof of the Lemma.**  \n   We construct the required family by an explicit Hall‑type assignment.\n\n   *Step 5.1 – choice of slopes.*  \n   Pick \\(m\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   s_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   (for instance \\(s_i=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\); none of them equals \\(-1\\) and only \\(s_1=0\\) would be forbidden, so we replace \\(s_1\\) by \\(2\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\) we shall determine a line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=s_i x+b_i\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   that passes through **exactly one** point of each anti‑diagonal \\(D_t\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le m+1\\)).  \n   Because the slope is different from \\(-1\\), a line can intersect a given diagonal in at most one point, so condition (4) is the strongest possible.\n\n   *Step 5.2 – the bipartite incidence graph.*  \n   For a fixed slope \\(s_i\\) and a diagonal \\(D_t\\) the intersection point of the line \\(y=s_i x+b\\) with the line \\(x+y=t\\) is obtained by solving  \n\n   \\[\n   x+s_i x+b=t\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x=\\frac{t-b}{1+s_i}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The point is an integer lattice point of \\(D_t\\) iff the right‑hand side of (5) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(t-1\\).  \n   For each diagonal \\(D_t\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   A_t=\\Bigl\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\;\\Big|\\;\n   \\frac{t-b_i}{1+s_i}\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   In other words, \\(i\\in A_t\\) exactly when the line \\(L_i\\) meets \\(D_t\\) in a lattice point.\n\n   *Step 5.3 – Hall’s condition.*  \n   We shall choose the intercepts \\(b_i\\) inductively so that for every diagonal \\(D_t\\) the set \\(A_t\\) has size exactly \\(t-1\\).  \n   Suppose we have already fixed \\(b_1,\\dots ,b_{i-1}\\) and that for each diagonal \\(D_t\\) the numbers of already‑chosen lines meeting it equal the number of points of \\(D_t\\) that we have already assigned.  \n   When we choose \\(b_i\\) we are free to pick any real number that does **not** make the line parallel to a previously used line and that does not create a new intersection with a diagonal at a point already used.  \n   Because each diagonal contains only finitely many lattice points, there are only finitely many “forbidden’’