nny line can contain at most one of the three points, and two sunny lines can cover at most two of them.  The third point would require a non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the requirement that exactly two lines be sunny.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(m\\ge 2\\) and consequently for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct for the case \\(m=2\\).*  The three points indeed force any line through two of them to be non\u2011sunny, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three.\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap.**  \nThe argument is then extended to *all* \\(m\\ge2\\) without justification.  For \\(m>2\\) the set \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) contains \\(\\mathcal Q_2\\) as a subset, but the remaining \\(m-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines (available because the total number of lines is \\(m\\)) could be used to cover the uncovered point of \\(\\mathcal Q_2\\).  Hence the conclusion \u201c\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(m\\ge2\\)\u201d does not follow from the reasoning given.  A more detailed argument (e.g. counting anti\u2011diagonals and showing that the non\u2011sunny lines are already forced to be the diagonal lines covering the larger anti\u2011diagonals) is required.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 6.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\n> \u201cTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  All are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  Each diagonal line covers exactly one anti\u2011diagonal; together they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  No line is sunny.\n\n#### 6.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and add any sunny line through the remaining point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-1\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The diagonal lines cover all points except \\((1,1)\\).  The line \\(y=2x-1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\neq0,-1\\), so it is sunny and distinct from the diagonal lines.  Hence the construction yields exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### 6.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(d=n-k\\).  \n> Employ the diagonal lines (1) for the \\(d\\) largest anti\u2011diagonals; they are non\u2011sunny.  The uncovered part is \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  \n> We now cover \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n> The following lemma provides the required covering.\u201d\n\n*Correct **provided** the reduction to \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) (step\u202f3) is valid.  Assuming that, the plan is sound.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Lemma (Sunny transversal)\n\n> **Lemma.** *Let \\(m\\ge 3\\). There exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\).*\n\n**Proof analysis.**\n\n1. **Base lines.**  \n   *\\(L_1: y=x\\), \\(L_2: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\\), \\(L_3: y=-2x+5\\).*  \n   All three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\); thus the lines are sunny.  \n   Direct substitution shows:\n   * \\(L_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\);\n   * \\(L_2\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\);\n   * \\(L_3\\) contains \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\).  \n   Hence the six points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\cup D_4\\) are covered.\n\n   *Correct.*\n\n2. **Remaining anti\u2011diagonals.**  \n   For each \\(t\\in\\{5,6,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) choose an irrational number \\(\\alpha_t\\) different from the three slopes already used and define  \n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y=\\alpha_t x+\\bigl(t-1-\\alpha_t\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n   \\]  \n   Substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=t-1\\); thus \\((1,t-1)\\in D_t\\) lies on \\(L_t\\).\n\n   *Correct construction.*\n\n3. **Uniqueness of integer points on \\(L_t\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(L_t\\) also contains another lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\).  \n   Then  \n   \\[\n   b=\\alpha_t a+(t-1-\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_t(a-1)+(t-1).\n   \\]  \n   Since \\(\\alpha_t\\) is irrational, \\(\\alpha_t(a-1)\\) is irrational unless \\(a-1=0\\).  Hence \\(a=1\\) and the only integer point on \\(L_t\\) is \\((1,t-1)\\).\n\n   *Correct reasoning.*\n\n4. **Counting lines.**  \n   The set of lines consists of the three base lines plus one line \\(L_t\\) for each \\(t=5,\\dots ,m+1\\).  \n   Number of \\(t\\)\u2019s: \\((m+1)-5+1=m-3\\).  \n   Total lines: \\(3+(m-3)=m\\).\n\n   *Correct.*\n\n5. **All lines are sunny and distinct.**  \n   The three base slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\); each \\(\\alpha_t\\) is irrational and chosen different from those three, so every line has a slope not equal to \\(0,-1\\) or undefined.  Distinctness follows because either the slopes differ or, if two \\(\\alpha_t\\) coincided, the intercepts differ (they depend on \\(t\\)).  \n\n   *Correct.*\n\nThus the lemma is proved rigorously.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Completion of the construction for \\(k\\ge3\\)\n\n> \u201cApplying the lemma with \\(m=k\\) gives \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the uncovered triangular set \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  Together with the \\(d=n-k\\) diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\u201d\n\n*Correct, assuming the reduction to \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) (step\u202f3) is valid.*\n\n---\n\n### 9. Conclusion  \n\n> \u201cAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n> \\[\n> k=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 3\\le k\\le n .\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct conclusion, matching the constructions and the (incomplete) impossibility argument.*\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment\n\nThe solution correctly identifies the set of admissible \\(k\\) and provides explicit constructions for all allowed values.  The lemma that supplies a sunny transversal for the triangular set \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) is rigorous.  However, two crucial reductions are used without proof:\n\n1. **Step\u202f2** \u2013 the claim that one may assume the diagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines lie on the *largest* anti\u2011diagonals.\n2. **Step\u202f4** \u2013 the extension of the impossibility argument from \\(m=2\\) to all \\(m\\ge2\\).\n\nThese gaps leave the logical chain incomplete, even though the final answer happens to be correct.  Consequently, the solution is **incomplete** due to the identified justification gaps."
