"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }k\\ge 3\\text{ with }k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) except \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   For \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=t\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (the anti\u2011diagonal of sum \\(t\\)).  \n   The set of points that must be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t ,\\qquad \n   |\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **A basic observation.**  \n   Two different points of the same anti\u2011diagonal have slope \\(-1\\); therefore a line that is **not** parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (in particular any sunny line) can contain **at most one** point of each \\(D_t\\).\n\n3. **Using diagonal lines.**  \n   A line of the form \\(x+y=t\\) (a \u201cdiagonal\u2019\u2019) contains *all* points of \\(D_t\\).  \n   Hence, if we decide to use \\(d\\) diagonal lines, we may take them for the\n   \\(d\\) *largest* anti\u2011diagonals, i.e. for  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n+1,\\;n,\\dots ,n-d+2 .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   After (1) the uncovered points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q_{m}= \\bigcup_{t=2}^{m+1}D_t ,\\qquad m:=n-d .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The set \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) is a triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(m\\); it\n   contains \\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) points and has exactly \\(m\\) anti\u2011diagonals\n   \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{m+1}\\).\n\n4. **Reduction of the problem.**  \n   The remaining \\(m=n-d\\) lines must cover \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\).  \n   Among those \\(m\\) lines we may have any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n   (the other \\(m-k\\) lines are horizontal, vertical or diagonal, all of which\n   are non\u2011sunny).  \n   Consequently the original question is equivalent to:\n\n   > **Problem \\(*\\).**  \n   > For a given integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) determine all \\(k\\) for which the\n   > triangular set \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct\n   > lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   For \\(m=2\\) the set \\(\\mathcal Q_2\\) consists of the three points  \n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   A sunny line cannot contain two of these points, because any line through\n   two of them is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence with two sunny lines at most two of the three points can be covered;\n   the third point would need a non\u2011sunny line.  Thus \\(k=2\\) is impossible for\n   every \\(m\\ge 2\\) and consequently for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n6. **Construction for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  \n   All are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and\n   add any sunny line through the remaining point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance\n   \\(y=2x-1\\)).  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n7. **A transversal of sunny lines for a triangular set.**  \n\n   > **Lemma (Sunny transversal).**  \n   > Let \\(m\\ge 3\\). There exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together\n   > contain every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   We treat the three smallest anti\u2011diagonals separately and then handle the\n   remaining \\(m-3\\) anti\u2011diagonals one by one.\n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 three base sunny lines covering \\(D_2,D_3,D_4\\).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y =  x      &&\\text{(slope }1),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 &&\\text{(slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x +5 &&\\text{(slope }-2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   All three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\); hence the lines\n   are sunny and distinct.  Direct substitution shows\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n   L_2\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n   L_3\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the six points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\cup D_4\\) are already covered.\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 one sunny line for each remaining anti\u2011diagonal.*  \n   For every \\(t\\in\\{5,6,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) choose an irrational number\n   \\(\\alpha_t\\) that is different from the three slopes already used.\n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_t:\\; y=\\alpha_t x+\\bigl(t-1-\\alpha_t\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(L_t\\) passes through the lattice point \\((1,t-1)\\in\n   D_t\\).  Suppose that \\(L_t\\) also contained another lattice point\n   \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\).  Then\n\n   \\[\n   b=\\alpha_t a+(t-1-\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_t (a-1)+(t-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\alpha_t\\) is irrational, the right\u2011hand side is irrational unless\n   \\(a=1\\); but \\(a=1\\) gives the already known point \\((1,t-1)\\).  Hence\n   \\(L_t\\) meets \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) **only** at \\((1,t-1)\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 counting.*  \n   We have produced the three base lines plus one line for each\n   \\(t=5,\\dots ,m+1\\); the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   3+(m-3)=m .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and all\n   lines are sunny.  \u220e\n\n8. **From the lemma to the original problem.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) or \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Set \\(d=n-k\\) (the number of diagonal, hence non\u2011sunny, lines).  \n   Use the diagonal lines (1) for the \\(d\\) largest anti\u2011diagonals; they are\n   non\u2011sunny.  The uncovered part is \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  \n   By the Lemma, \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct\n   sunny lines.  Altogether we have \\(d+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.\n\n   The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible by the argument of step\u202f5.  Hence the set of\n   admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }k\\ge 3\\text{ with }k\\le n\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b=t\\},\\qquad\nt=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe required points are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{n+1}D_t ,\\qquad \n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A line that is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) meets each \\(D_t\\) in at most one point\n\nIndeed, any two distinct points of the same \\(D_t\\) have slope \\(-1\\); a line\nwith a different slope cannot contain both of them.\n\nConsequently a **sunny** line (slope \\(\\neq0,-1\\) and not vertical) can\ncontribute at most one point to each anti\u2011diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Using diagonal lines\n\nA diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) contains all points of \\(D_t\\).  \nIf we decide to use \\(d\\) diagonal lines, we may as well choose them for the\n\\(d\\) *largest* anti\u2011diagonals, namely for the values of \\(t\\) listed in (1)\nabove.  After that the uncovered points form the triangular set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal Q_m=\\bigcup_{t=2}^{m+1}D_t ,\\qquad m:=n-d .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) has exactly \\(m\\) anti\u2011diagonals and\n\\(\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction\n\nThe remaining \\(m=n-d\\) lines must cover \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\).  \nAmong those \\(m\\) lines let \\(k\\) be sunny; the other \\(m-k\\) are horizontal,\nvertical or diagonal (hence non\u2011sunny).  Therefore the original problem is\nequivalent to the following.\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  \n> For a given integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) determine all \\(k\\) for which the set\n> \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) can be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct lines, exactly\n> \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nFor \\(m=2\\) we have \\(\\mathcal Q_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points is either horizontal, vertical or has\nslope \\(-1\\); hence it is **not** sunny.  Thus a sunny line can contain at\nmost one of the three points, and two sunny lines can cover at most two of\nthem.  The third point would require a non\u2011sunny line, contradicting the\nrequirement that exactly two lines be sunny.  Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for\nevery \\(m\\ge 2\\) and consequently for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\n#### 5.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\).  All are non\u2011sunny,\nso \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n#### 5.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\) and add any sunny\nline through the remaining point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-1\\)).  Exactly\none line is sunny.\n\n#### 5.3  \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(d=n-k\\).  \nEmploy the diagonal lines (1) for the \\(d\\) largest anti\u2011diagonals; they are\nnon\u2011sunny.  The uncovered part is \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).\n\nWe now cover \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe following lemma provides the required covering.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma (Sunny transversal)\n\n*Let \\(m\\ge 3\\). There exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains\nevery point of \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the three smallest anti\u2011diagonals separately.\n\n*Base lines.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y =  x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x +5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,-1\\}\\); hence the lines are\nsunny and distinct.  A direct check shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\nL_2\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nL_3\\cap\\mathcal Q_m &\\supset\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the six points of \\(D_2\\cup D_3\\cup D_4\\) are already covered.\n\n*Remaining anti\u2011diagonals.*  \nFor each \\(t\\in\\{5,6,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) choose an irrational number\n\\(\\alpha_t\\) different from the three slopes already used and define\n\n\\[\nL_t:\\; y=\\alpha_t x+\\bigl(t-1-\\alpha_t\\bigr). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) forces \\(L_t\\) to pass through the lattice point \\((1,t-1)\\in\nD_t\\).  Suppose that \\(L_t\\) also contained another lattice point\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\).  Then\n\n\\[\nb=\\alpha_t a+(t-1-\\alpha_t)=\\alpha_t (a-1)+(t-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\alpha_t\\) is irrational, the right\u2011hand side is irrational unless\n\\(a=1\\); the case \\(a=1\\) yields the already known point \\((1,t-1)\\).\nHence \\(L_t\\) meets \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) **only** at \\((1,t-1)\\).\n\n*Counting.*  \nWe have the three base lines and one line for each\n\\(t=5,\\dots ,m+1\\); the total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n3+(m-3)=m .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal Q_m\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and all\nlines are sunny.  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplying the lemma with \\(m=k\\) gives \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the\nuncovered triangular set \\(\\mathcal Q_k\\).  Together with the \\(d=n-k\\)\ndiagonal (non\u2011sunny) lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are\n\n\\[\nk=0,\\qquad k=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad 3\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe value \\(k=2\\) is impossible, as shown in \u00a74.  Hence the answer to the\noriginal problem is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,\\;k=1,\\;\\text{or }k\\ge 3\\text{ with }k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
